# Chapter 25 Motion of charged particles — part b: 25.4 and 25.5.
QE, ME, MP = 1.60e-19, 9.11e-31, 1.67e-27
EM = 1.76e11
MU0 = 4 * pi * 1e-7

# ═════════════════════════ 25.4 The Hall effect (17/19/8) ═════════════════════════
lesson('25.4')
M('E', 1, 'What causes a Hall voltage across a current-carrying slab in a magnetic field?', 'The magnetic force pushes the moving charge carriers to one side of the slab', ['The current heats one side of the slab', 'The magnetic field creates new charges', 'The slab becomes a permanent magnet'], 'Charge builds up on one face, giving a p.d. across the slab.')
N('E', 2, 'A Hall probe gives 0.40 mV in a certain field. The flux density is doubled, with the same current. What is the new Hall voltage, in mV?', 0.80, 'mV',
  'V_H ∝ B: 2 × 0.40 = 0.80 mV', ['C1: V_H ∝ B'], sig=2, aq='V_H')
M('E', 1, 'With a constant current, which describes a graph of Hall voltage against magnetic flux density?', 'A straight line through the origin', ['A curve that levels off', 'A horizontal line', 'A straight line with a negative gradient'], 'V_H = BI/ntq, so V_H ∝ B.')
N('E', 2, 'A semiconductor slice has 1.0 × 10²² charge carriers per m³ and is 1.0 mm thick. It carries 10 mA in a field of 0.10 T. Calculate the Hall voltage, in mV, using V_H = BI/ntq.' + take('e'), 0.10 * 10e-3 / (1.0e22 * 1.0e-3 * QE) * 1000, 'mV',
  f'V_H = 0.10 × 10 × 10⁻³ ÷ (1.0 × 10²² × 1.0 × 10⁻³ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹) = {sf(1e-3 / (1e22 * 1e-3 * QE))} V. In mV: {sf(1e-3 / (1e22 * 1e-3 * QE), 4)} × 1000 = {sf(1e-3 / (1e22 * 1e-3 * QE) * 1000)}', ['C1: V_H = BI/ntq'], aq='V_H')
M('E', 1, 'Why are Hall probes made as thin slices?', 'The Hall voltage is inversely proportional to the thickness, so a thin slice gives a larger voltage', ['A thin slice carries a larger current', 'Thin slices have more charge carriers', 'A thin slice is not affected by the field'], 'V_H = BI/ntq, so for a given B and I the Hall voltage is inversely proportional to the thickness t.')
N('E', 2, 'A Hall probe gives 5.0 mV in a field of 0.20 T. In another field, with the same current, it gives 12.5 mV. Calculate this field.', 0.20 * 12.5 / 5.0, 'T',
  'B ∝ V_H: B = 0.20 × 12.5 ÷ 5.0 = 0.50 T', ['C1: proportionality'], sig=2, aq='B')
M('E', 1, 'How should a Hall probe be held to give the largest reading?', 'With its flat face at right angles to the magnetic field', ['With its flat face parallel to the field', 'Pointing along the field', 'At 45° to the field'], 'Only the field component perpendicular to the slice deflects the carriers across it.')
N('E', 2, 'A copper wire of cross-sectional area 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ m² carries 2.0 A. Copper has 8.5 × 10²⁸ free electrons per m³. Calculate the drift speed of the electrons.' + take('e'), 2.0 / (8.5e28 * 1.0e-6 * QE), 'm s⁻¹',
  f'v = I/(nAq) = 2.0 ÷ (8.5 × 10²⁸ × 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹) = {sf(2 / (8.5e28 * 1e-6 * QE))} m s⁻¹', ['C1: I = nAvq'], aq='v')
M('E', 1, 'What can the sign of the Hall voltage tell us about a material?', 'Whether its charge carriers are positive or negative', ['Its density', 'Its temperature', 'Its resistance'], 'Positive and negative carriers moving to give the same current are pushed to the same side, giving opposite polarities.')
N('E', 2, 'Charge carriers drift at 0.20 m s⁻¹ through a semiconductor slice 5.0 mm wide in a field of 0.40 T. Using E_H = vB, calculate the Hall voltage across the slice, in mV.', 0.20 * 0.40 * 5.0e-3 * 1000, 'mV',
  'E_H = vB = 0.20 × 0.40 = 0.080 V m⁻¹. V_H = E_H × width = 0.080 × 5.0 × 10⁻³ = 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ V. In mV: 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ × 1000 = 0.40', ['C1: E_H = vB then × width'], sig=2, aq='V_H')
M('E', 1, 'Why is the Hall voltage in a metal very small?', 'Metals have a very large number of free electrons per m³, so they drift very slowly', ['Metals have no free electrons', 'Metals are not affected by magnetic fields', 'Metals are always very thick'], 'V_H = BI/ntq: a large n gives a small V_H.')
N('E', 1, 'A Hall probe gives 3.0 mV with a probe current of 20 mA. What does it give, in the same field, with a current of 30 mA, in mV?', 4.5, 'mV', 'V_H ∝ I: 3.0 × 30 ÷ 20 = 4.5 mV', [], sig=2, aq='V_H')
M('E', 1, 'In the steady state in a Hall probe, which forces on a moving charge carrier are balanced?', 'The magnetic force Bqv and the electric force qE_H from the Hall field', ['The magnetic force and the carrier\'s weight', 'The electric force and gravity', 'The current and the voltage'], 'Carriers then flow straight along the slice: qE_H = Bqv.')
N('E', 2, 'A Hall probe 0.50 mm thick carries 20 mA in a field of 0.50 T and gives a Hall voltage of 2.5 mV. Calculate the number of charge carriers per m³.' + take('e'), 0.50 * 0.020 / (2.5e-3 * 0.50e-3 * QE), 'm⁻³',
  'n = BI/(V_H t q) = 0.50 × 0.020 ÷ (2.5 × 10⁻³ × 0.50 × 10⁻³ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹) = 5.0 × 10²² m⁻³', ['C1: rearranged V_H = BI/ntq'], sig=2, aq='n')
M('E', 1, 'Why must the current through a Hall probe be kept constant while it is used?', 'The Hall voltage is proportional to the current as well as to B', ['Otherwise the probe overheats', 'The field depends on the current', 'The thickness depends on the current'], 'V_H = BI/ntq: a change in I would be mistaken for a change in B.')
N('E', 2, 'A copper strip 0.10 mm thick carries 5.0 A at right angles to a 1.0 T field. Copper has 8.5 × 10²⁸ free electrons per m³. Calculate the Hall voltage, in µV.' + take('e'), 1.0 * 5.0 / (8.5e28 * 0.10e-3 * QE) * 1e6, 'µV',
  f'V_H = BI/ntq = 1.0 × 5.0 ÷ (8.5 × 10²⁸ × 0.10 × 10⁻³ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹) = {sf(5 / (8.5e28 * 1e-4 * QE))} V. In µV: {sf(5 / (8.5e28 * 1e-4 * QE), 4)} × 10⁶ = {sf(5 / (8.5e28 * 1e-4 * QE) * 1e6)}', ['C1: V_H = BI/ntq'], aq='V_H')
M('E', 1, 'The magnetic field through a Hall probe is reversed. What happens to the Hall voltage?', 'It reverses polarity but keeps the same size', ['It doubles', 'It becomes zero', 'It stays exactly the same'], 'The carriers are pushed to the opposite face.')
# medium
N('M', 3, 'A Hall probe gives 0.150 V in a field of 0.30 T. With the same current, it gives 0.085 V in an unknown field. Calculate the unknown flux density.', 0.30 * 0.085 / 0.150, 'T',
  f'B ∝ V_H: B = 0.30 × 0.085 ÷ 0.150 = {sf(0.3 * 0.085 / 0.15)} T', ['C1: V_H ∝ B', 'C1: substitution'], aq='B')
N('M', 4, 'A semiconductor Hall probe has 2.0 × 10²¹ carriers per m³ and is 0.20 mm thick. It carries 5.0 mA in a field of 0.25 T. Calculate the Hall voltage, in mV.' + take('e'), 0.25 * 5.0e-3 / (2.0e21 * 0.20e-3 * QE) * 1000, 'mV',
  f'V_H = 0.25 × 5.0 × 10⁻³ ÷ (2.0 × 10²¹ × 0.20 × 10⁻³ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹) = {sf(1.25e-3 / (2e21 * 2e-4 * QE))} V. In mV: {sf(1.25e-3 / (2e21 * 2e-4 * QE), 4)} × 1000 = {sf(1.25e-3 / (2e21 * 2e-4 * QE) * 1000)}', ['C1: V_H = BI/ntq', 'C1: units converted', 'C1: value'], aq='V_H')
M('M', 3, 'A Hall probe is replaced by one of the same material but twice as thick, with the same current and field. What happens to the Hall voltage?', 'It halves', ['It doubles', 'It stays the same', 'It quadruples'], 'V_H = BI/ntq ∝ 1/t, so doubling the thickness halves the Hall voltage.', ['C1: V_H = BI/ntq', 'C1: inverse with t'])
N('M', 3, 'A semiconductor slice 5.0 mm wide and 0.20 mm thick carries 5.0 mA and has 2.0 × 10²¹ carriers per m³. Calculate the drift speed of the carriers.' + take('e'), 5.0e-3 / (2.0e21 * 5.0e-3 * 0.20e-3 * QE), 'm s⁻¹',
  f'A = 5.0 × 10⁻³ × 0.20 × 10⁻³ = 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ m². v = I/(nAq) = 5.0 × 10⁻³ ÷ (2.0 × 10²¹ × 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹) = {sf(5e-3 / (2e21 * 1e-6 * QE))} m s⁻¹', ['C1: cross-sectional area', 'C1: v = I/(nAq)'], aq='v')
N('M', 3, 'Carriers drift at 15.6 m s⁻¹ through a semiconductor slice in a field of 0.25 T. Calculate the Hall field E_H.', 15.6 * 0.25, 'V m⁻¹',
  'E_H = vB = 15.6 × 0.25 = 3.9 V m⁻¹', ['M1: qE_H = Bqv', 'C1: E_H = vB'], sig=2, aq='E_H')
M('M', 3, 'Two Hall probes have the same size and current. Probe A has 100 times as many charge carriers per m³ as probe B. What is (Hall voltage of A) ÷ (Hall voltage of B)?', '1/100', ['100', '1', '10'], 'V_H = BI/ntq ∝ 1/n, so 100 times the carrier density gives 1/100 of the voltage.', ['C1: V_H = BI/ntq', 'C1: inverse with n'])
N('M', 3, 'A wire of cross-sectional area 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ m² carries 1.0 A, and its electrons drift at 1.0 × 10⁻⁴ m s⁻¹. Calculate the number of free electrons per m³.' + take('e'), 1.0 / (1.0e-6 * 1.0e-4 * QE), 'm⁻³',
  f'n = I/(Avq) = 1.0 ÷ (1.0 × 10⁻⁶ × 1.0 × 10⁻⁴ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹) = {sf(1 / (1e-10 * QE))} m⁻³', ['C1: I = nAvq', 'C1: rearranged'], aq='n')
N('M', 4, 'A Hall probe reads 3.0 mV when its face is at right angles to a field. It is then turned so that the normal to its face is at 40° to the field. Calculate the new reading, in mV.', 3.0 * cos(radians(40)), 'mV',
  f'Only the perpendicular component B cos 40° acts: V = 3.0 × cos 40° = {sf(3 * cos(radians(40)))} mV', ['M1: perpendicular component', 'C1: × cos 40°', 'C1: value'], aq='V_H')
M('M', 4, 'Identical currents flow along an n-type and a p-type semiconductor slice in the same field. How do the Hall voltages compare?', 'They have opposite polarities, because the carriers have opposite signs', ['They have the same polarity', 'Only the n-type gives a Hall voltage', 'Both are zero'], 'Holes and electrons moving to give the same current are pushed to the same face but carry opposite charges.', ['C1: same side deflection', 'C1: opposite charge', 'C1: opposite polarity'])
N('M', 3, 'Copper has density 8960 kg m⁻³ and molar mass 0.0635 kg mol⁻¹, with one free electron per atom. Calculate the number of free electrons per m³.' + take('NA'), 8960 / 0.0635 * 6.02e23, 'm⁻³',
  f'n = ρN_A/M = 8960 × 6.02 × 10²³ ÷ 0.0635 = {sf(8960 * 6.02e23 / 0.0635)} m⁻³', ['C1: moles per m³', 'C1: × N_A'], aq='n')
N('M', 3, 'A sodium strip (2.5 × 10²⁸ free electrons per m³) is 0.050 mm thick and carries 1.0 A in a field of 1.0 T. Calculate the Hall voltage, in µV.' + take('e'), 1.0 * 1.0 / (2.5e28 * 0.050e-3 * QE) * 1e6, 'µV',
  'V_H = 1.0 × 1.0 ÷ (2.5 × 10²⁸ × 0.050 × 10⁻³ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹) = 5.0 × 10⁻⁶ V. In µV: 5.0 × 10⁻⁶ × 10⁶ = 5.0', ['C1: V_H = BI/ntq', 'C1: conversion'], sig=2, aq='V_H')
M('M', 3, 'Why must a Hall probe be calibrated before use?', 'Its carrier density and thickness are not known accurately, and its output can vary with temperature', ['Its current changes the field it measures', 'The Hall voltage does not depend on B', 'The probe stores charge'], 'Calibration in a known field gives the voltage per unit B directly.', ['C1: n and t uncertain', 'C1: compare with known field'])
N('M', 3, 'A Hall probe gives 1.0 mV in a field of 10 mT. In the Earth\'s field it gives 5.0 µV. Calculate the Earth\'s field, in µT.', 10e-3 * 5.0e-6 / 1.0e-3 * 1e6, 'µT',
  'B ∝ V_H: B = 10 × 10⁻³ × 5.0 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ 1.0 × 10⁻³ = 5.0 × 10⁻⁵ T. In µT: 5.0 × 10⁻⁵ × 10⁶ = 50', ['C1: proportionality', 'C1: conversion'], sig=2, aq='B')
N('M', 4, 'A germanium Hall probe has 1.0 × 10²² carriers per m³ and is 0.50 mm thick. Calculate the current needed to give a Hall voltage of 1.0 mV in a field of 0.20 T, in mA.' + take('e'), 1.0e-3 * 1.0e22 * 0.50e-3 * QE / 0.20 * 1000, 'mA',
  'I = V_H n t q ÷ B = 1.0 × 10⁻³ × 1.0 × 10²² × 0.50 × 10⁻³ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ ÷ 0.20 = 4.0 × 10⁻³ A. In mA: 4.0 × 10⁻³ × 1000 = 4.0', ['C1: rearranged', 'C1: substitution', 'C1: conversion'], sig=2, aq='I')
M('M', 3, 'Which change does NOT alter the Hall voltage of a probe?', 'Making the slice wider, with the same thickness, current and field', ['Making the slice thinner', 'Increasing the current', 'Increasing the flux density'], 'V_H = BI/ntq does not contain the width: a wider slice has a smaller drift speed but a larger width.', ['C1: V_H independent of width', 'C1: v falls as width rises'])
N('M', 3, 'Calculate (Hall voltage in a semiconductor with 1.0 × 10²² carriers per m³) ÷ (Hall voltage in copper with 8.5 × 10²⁸ carriers per m³), for the same size, current and field.', 8.5e28 / 1.0e22, None,
  'V_H ∝ 1/n: ratio = 8.5 × 10²⁸ ÷ 1.0 × 10²² = 8.5 × 10⁶', ['C1: V_H ∝ 1/n', 'C1: ratio'], sig=2, aq='ratio')
N('M', 3, 'In a Hall probe, the Hall field is 4.0 V m⁻¹. Calculate the electric force on a carrier of charge e, in units of 10⁻¹⁹ N.' + take('e'), QE * 4.0, '10⁻¹⁹ N',
  f'F = eE_H = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 4.0 = {sf(QE * 4)} N', ['C1: F = qE', 'C1: value'], sig=2, aq='F')
M('M', 3, 'Across which faces of a rectangular Hall probe is the Hall voltage measured?', 'The pair of faces at right angles to both the current and the magnetic field', ['The faces through which the current enters and leaves', 'The faces at right angles to the field only', 'Any two opposite faces'], 'The carriers are deflected along the direction of I × B.', ['C1: perpendicular to I', 'C1: perpendicular to B'])
N('M', 3, 'Electrons drift at 1.5 × 10⁻⁴ m s⁻¹ in a copper strip at right angles to a field of 0.80 T. Calculate the magnetic force on one electron, in units of 10⁻²³ N.' + take('e'), QE * 1.5e-4 * 0.80, '10⁻²³ N',
  f'F = Bev = 0.80 × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 1.5 × 10⁻⁴ = {sf(0.8 * QE * 1.5e-4)} N', ['C1: F = Bqv', 'C1: value'], aq='F')
# hard
N('H', 6, '(a) Starting from qE_H = Bqv and I = nAvq, show that the Hall voltage across a slice of width d and thickness t is V_H = BI/ntq. (b) Calculate V_H for a slice with 1.5 × 10²² carriers per m³, thickness 0.30 mm, current 8.0 mA and field 0.60 T, in mV. Give only the answer to (b).' + take('e'), 0.60 * 8.0e-3 / (1.5e22 * 0.30e-3 * QE) * 1000, 'mV',
  f'(a) E_H = V_H/d = vB, and v = I/(nAq) = I/(ndtq), so V_H = Bvd = BI/(ntq). (b) V_H = 0.60 × 8.0 × 10⁻³ ÷ (1.5 × 10²² × 0.30 × 10⁻³ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹) = {sf(4.8e-3 / (1.5e22 * 3e-4 * QE))} V. In mV: {sf(4.8e-3 / (1.5e22 * 3e-4 * QE), 4)} × 1000 = {sf(4.8e-3 / (1.5e22 * 3e-4 * QE) * 1000)}',
  ['M1: E_H = V_H/d = vB', 'M1: v = I/(nAq), A = dt', 'B1: result shown', 'C1: substitution', 'C1: conversion'], aq='V_H')
N('H', 5, 'A Hall probe 0.20 mm thick carries 10 mA. Readings: B/T = 0.10, 0.20, 0.30, 0.40 and V_H/mV = 1.1, 2.2, 3.3, 4.4. (a) Calculate the gradient of the V_H–B graph. (b) Use the gradient to calculate the number of charge carriers per m³. Give only the answer to (b).' + take('e'), 10e-3 / ((4.4e-3 - 1.1e-3) / 0.30 * 0.20e-3 * QE), 'm⁻³',
  f'(a) Gradient = (4.4 − 1.1) × 10⁻³ ÷ (0.40 − 0.10) = {sf(3.3e-3 / 0.3)} V T⁻¹. (b) Gradient = I/(ntq), so n = I ÷ (gradient × t × q) = 10 × 10⁻³ ÷ ({sf(3.3e-3 / 0.3, 4)} × 0.20 × 10⁻³ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹) = {sf(10e-3 / (3.3e-3 / 0.3 * 2e-4 * QE))} m⁻³',
  ['C1: gradient', 'M1: gradient = I/(ntq)', 'C1: rearranged', 'C1: value'], aq='n')
N('H', 6, 'Two Hall probes are each 0.10 mm thick and carry 0.10 A in a field of 0.50 T. One is copper (8.5 × 10²⁸ electrons per m³) and the other indium antimonide (2.0 × 10²² carriers per m³). (a) Calculate the Hall voltage for copper. (b) Calculate the Hall voltage for indium antimonide, in mV. (c) Explain which is more suitable for a practical probe. Give only the answer to (b).' + take('e'), 0.50 * 0.10 / (2.0e22 * 0.10e-3 * QE) * 1000, 'mV',
  f'(a) V_Cu = 0.50 × 0.10 ÷ (8.5 × 10²⁸ × 0.10 × 10⁻³ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹) = {sf(0.05 / (8.5e28 * 1e-4 * QE))} V. (b) V_InSb = 0.50 × 0.10 ÷ (2.0 × 10²² × 0.10 × 10⁻³ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹) = {sf(0.05 / (2e22 * 1e-4 * QE))} V. In mV: {sf(0.05 / (2e22 * 1e-4 * QE), 4)} × 1000 = {sf(0.05 / (2e22 * 1e-4 * QE) * 1000)}. (c) Indium antimonide: its voltage is millions of times larger and easy to measure.',
  ['C1: copper value', 'C1: InSb value', 'C1: conversion', 'B1: semiconductor much larger V_H', 'B1: easily measured'], aq='V_H')
N('H', 5, 'A semiconductor slice is 4.0 mm wide and 0.25 mm thick, carries 2.0 mA and has 5.0 × 10²¹ carriers per m³. It is in a field of 0.40 T. (a) Calculate the drift speed of the carriers. (b) Calculate the Hall field. (c) Calculate the Hall voltage, in mV. Give only the answer to (c).' + take('e'), 2.0e-3 / (5.0e21 * 4.0e-3 * 0.25e-3 * QE) * 0.40 * 4.0e-3 * 1000, 'mV',
  f'(a) v = 2.0 × 10⁻³ ÷ (5.0 × 10²¹ × 4.0 × 10⁻³ × 0.25 × 10⁻³ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹) = {sf(2e-3 / (5e21 * 1e-6 * QE))} m s⁻¹. (b) E_H = vB = {sf(2e-3 / (5e21 * 1e-6 * QE), 4)} × 0.40 = {sf(2e-3 / (5e21 * 1e-6 * QE) * 0.4)} V m⁻¹. (c) V_H = E_H × width = {sf(2e-3 / (5e21 * 1e-6 * QE) * 0.4, 4)} × 4.0 × 10⁻³ = {sf(2e-3 / (5e21 * 1e-6 * QE) * 0.4 * 4e-3)} V. In mV: {sf(2e-3 / (5e21 * 1e-6 * QE) * 0.4 * 4e-3, 4)} × 1000 = {sf(2e-3 / (5e21 * 1e-6 * QE) * 0.4 * 4e-3 * 1000)}',
  ['C1: v = I/(nAq)', 'C1: E_H = vB', 'C1: V_H = E_H d', 'C1: conversion'], aq='V_H')
N('H', 6, 'A calibrated Hall probe gives 30 mV in a test field of 1.0 mT. Outdoors, it reads 1.4 mV when held to measure the vertical component of the Earth\'s field and 0.60 mV for the horizontal component. (a) Calculate both components. (b) Calculate the total field, in µT. (c) Calculate the angle of dip. Give only the answer to (c).', degrees(atan(1.4 / 0.60)), '°',
  f'(a) B_v = 1.4 ÷ 30 × 1.0 × 10⁻³ = {sf(1.4 / 30 * 1e-3)} T; B_h = 0.60 ÷ 30 × 1.0 × 10⁻³ = 2.0 × 10⁻⁵ T. (b) B = √(({sf(1.4 / 30 * 1e-3, 4)})² + (2.0 × 10⁻⁵)²) = {sf(sqrt((1.4 / 30 * 1e-3) ** 2 + 4e-10))} T, which is {sf(sqrt((1.4 / 30 * 1e-3) ** 2 + 4e-10) * 1e6)} µT. (c) tan θ = 1.4 ÷ 0.60, so θ = tan⁻¹ 2.333 = {sf(degrees(atan(1.4 / 0.6)))}°',
  ['C1: calibration factor', 'C1: components', 'C1: total field', 'M1: tan dip = B_v/B_h', 'C1: angle'], aq='dip')
N('H', 5, 'A semiconductor Hall probe gives 5.0 mV in a certain field at room temperature. When it warms up, its carrier density increases by 20 %. (a) Explain why the Hall voltage changes. (b) Calculate the new Hall voltage, in mV, if B and I are unchanged. (c) Suggest why probes are recalibrated regularly. Give only the answer to (b).', 5.0 / 1.20, 'mV',
  f'(a) V_H = BI/ntq, so V_H ∝ 1/n. (b) V_H = 5.0 ÷ 1.20 = {sf(5 / 1.2)} mV. (c) Temperature changes alter n, so the voltage for a given B drifts.',
  ['B1: V_H ∝ 1/n', 'C1: ÷ 1.20', 'C1: value', 'B1: temperature dependence'], aq='V_H')
N('H', 6, 'A p-type semiconductor slice 0.15 mm thick carries 6.0 mA in a field of 0.30 T and gives a Hall voltage of 2.4 mV. (a) Explain how the polarity of the Hall voltage shows that the carriers are positive holes. (b) Calculate the density of holes. Give only the answer to (b).' + take('e'), 0.30 * 6.0e-3 / (2.4e-3 * 0.15e-3 * QE), 'm⁻³',
  f'(a) Positive holes moving along the current are pushed by the magnetic force to the same face that electrons would reach, but they make that face positive rather than negative. (b) p = BI/(V_H t e) = 0.30 × 6.0 × 10⁻³ ÷ (2.4 × 10⁻³ × 0.15 × 10⁻³ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹) = {sf(1.8e-3 / (2.4e-3 * 1.5e-4 * QE))} m⁻³',
  ['B1: same side deflection', 'B1: opposite polarity for positive carriers', 'M1: rearranged V_H = BI/ntq', 'C1: substitution', 'C1: value'], aq='p')
N('H', 5, 'A long solenoid has 3000 turns per metre and carries 2.0 A, so that B = µ₀nI. A Hall probe inside it has 1.0 × 10²² carriers per m³, is 0.20 mm thick and carries 20 mA. (a) Calculate the field in the solenoid. (b) Calculate the expected Hall voltage, in mV. Give only the answer to (b).' + take('mu0', 'e'), MU0 * 3000 * 2.0 * 20e-3 / (1.0e22 * 0.20e-3 * QE) * 1000, 'mV',
  f'(a) B = 4π × 10⁻⁷ × 3000 × 2.0 = {sf(MU0 * 6000)} T. (b) V_H = {sf(MU0 * 6000, 4)} × 20 × 10⁻³ ÷ (1.0 × 10²² × 0.20 × 10⁻³ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹) = {sf(MU0 * 6000 * 0.02 / (1e22 * 2e-4 * QE))} V. In mV: {sf(MU0 * 6000 * 0.02 / (1e22 * 2e-4 * QE), 4)} × 1000 = {sf(MU0 * 6000 * 0.02 / (1e22 * 2e-4 * QE) * 1000)}',
  ['C1: B = µ₀nI', 'C1: V_H = BI/ntq', 'C1: substitution', 'C1: conversion'], aq='V_H')

# ═════════════════════════ 25.5 Discovering the electron (17/19/8) ═════════════════════════
lesson('25.5')
M('E', 1, 'What is thermionic emission?', 'The release of electrons from the surface of a heated metal', ['The emission of light by a hot gas', 'The release of electrons when light shines on a metal', 'The emission of α-particles from a hot source'], 'Heating gives some free electrons enough energy to escape the metal.')
N('E', 2, 'In a fine-beam tube, electrons are accelerated from rest through 300 V. Using e/m = 1.76 × 10¹¹ C kg⁻¹, find their speed.', sqrt(2 * EM * 300), 'm s⁻¹',
  f'½mv² = eV gives v = √(2(e/m)V) = √(2 × 1.76 × 10¹¹ × 300) = {sf(sqrt(2 * EM * 300))} m s⁻¹', ['C1: v = √(2(e/m)V)'], aq='v')
M('E', 1, 'Cathode rays passing between two charged plates bend towards the positive plate. What does this show?', 'The rays carry negative charge', ['The rays carry positive charge', 'The rays are uncharged', 'The rays are electromagnetic waves'], 'Opposite charges attract.')
N('E', 2, 'An electron is accelerated from rest through 250 V. Calculate the kinetic energy it gains, in units of 10⁻¹⁷ J.' + take('e'), QE * 250, '10⁻¹⁷ J',
  f'E_k = eV = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 250 = {sf(QE * 250)} J (250 eV)', ['C1: E_k = eV'], sig=2, aq='E_k')
M('E', 1, 'Thomson found that cathode rays had the same e/m whatever metal the cathode was made of. What did he conclude?', 'The particles are a common constituent of all atoms', ['Each metal produces a different particle', 'The rays are a type of light', 'The particles are positive ions'], 'A universal e/m suggests the same particle comes from every element.')
N('E', 2, 'Using e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C and mₑ = 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg, calculate the specific charge e/m of the electron, in units of 10¹¹ C kg⁻¹.', QE / ME, '10¹¹ C kg⁻¹',
  f'e/m = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ ÷ 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ = {sf(QE / ME)} C kg⁻¹', ['C1: e ÷ m'], aq='e/m')
M('E', 1, 'Why must the tube in a cathode-ray or fine-beam experiment contain very little gas?', 'So that the electrons are not stopped or scattered by collisions with gas molecules', ['So that the glass does not break', 'So that the electrons can be seen more easily', 'Because gas would absorb the magnetic field'], 'At low pressure the mean free path is long enough for a visible beam.')
N('E', 2, 'A beam of electrons moving at 1.0 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹ is bent into a circle of radius 5.7 cm by a field of 1.0 mT. Deduce e/m, in units of 10¹¹ C kg⁻¹.', 1.0e7 / (1.0e-3 * 0.057), '10¹¹ C kg⁻¹',
  f'e/m = v/(Br) = 1.0 × 10⁷ ÷ (1.0 × 10⁻³ × 0.057) = {sf(1e7 / (1e-3 * 0.057))} C kg⁻¹', ['C1: e/m = v/(Br)'], aq='e/m')
M('E', 1, 'Why can the path of the electrons be seen in a fine-beam tube?', 'The electrons excite atoms of the low-pressure gas, which then emit light', ['The electrons themselves glow', 'The magnetic field glows', 'The glass becomes hot'], 'Collisions excite the gas atoms; they emit light as they return to lower energy levels.')
N('E', 2, 'Calculate the accelerating p.d. needed to give electrons a speed of 2.0 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹, using e/m = 1.76 × 10¹¹ C kg⁻¹.', (2.0e7) ** 2 / (2 * EM), 'V',
  f'V = v²/(2e/m) = (2.0 × 10⁷)² ÷ (2 × 1.76 × 10¹¹) = {sf(4e14 / (2 * EM))} V', ['C1: rearranged ½mv² = eV'], aq='V')
M('E', 1, 'What did Millikan\'s oil-drop experiment measure directly?', 'The charges on individual oil drops, which were always multiples of e', ['The mass of the electron', 'The speed of the electron', 'The ratio e/m'], 'Balancing the drops gave their charges, all whole-number multiples of 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.')
N('E', 2, 'Calculate the specific charge of the proton, e/mₚ, in units of 10⁷ C kg⁻¹.' + take('e', 'mp'), QE / MP, '10⁷ C kg⁻¹',
  f'e/mₚ = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ ÷ 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ = {sf(QE / MP)} C kg⁻¹', ['C1: e ÷ mₚ'], aq='e/m')
M('E', 1, 'Thomson\'s value of e/m for cathode-ray particles was about 1800 times that for hydrogen ions. Assuming equal charges, what did this suggest?', 'The particles are about 1800 times lighter than a hydrogen ion', ['The particles are 1800 times heavier than a hydrogen ion', 'The particles carry 1800 times more charge', 'The particles are hydrogen ions'], 'With the same e, a larger e/m means a smaller mass.')
N('E', 1, 'Using e/m = 1.76 × 10¹¹ C kg⁻¹ for the electron and 9.58 × 10⁷ C kg⁻¹ for the proton, calculate (e/m for the electron) ÷ (e/m for the proton).', 1.76e11 / 9.58e7, None,
  f'Ratio = 1.76 × 10¹¹ ÷ 9.58 × 10⁷ = {sf(1.76e11 / 9.58e7)}', [], aq='ratio')
M('E', 1, 'Which arrangement did Thomson use to find the speed of the cathode-ray particles?', 'Crossed electric and magnetic fields adjusted until the beam was undeflected, so v = E/B', ['A magnetic field alone, measuring the radius', 'An electric field alone, measuring the deflection', 'A stopwatch and a ruler'], 'Balancing eE = Bev gives v = E/B.')
N('E', 2, 'In Thomson\'s apparatus a beam is undeflected when E = 1.5 × 10⁴ V m⁻¹ and B = 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ T. Calculate the speed of the particles.', 1.5e4 / 5.0e-4, 'm s⁻¹',
  'v = E/B = 1.5 × 10⁴ ÷ 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ = 3.0 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹', ['C1: v = E/B'], sig=2, aq='v')
M('E', 1, 'In an electron gun, why is the anode at a high positive potential relative to the cathode?', 'To attract and accelerate the emitted electrons towards it', ['To heat the cathode', 'To repel the electrons back to the cathode', 'To produce a magnetic field'], 'The p.d. does work eV on each electron.')
# medium
N('M', 3, 'In a fine-beam tube, electrons accelerated through 250 V follow a circle of radius 4.4 cm in a field of 1.2 mT. Calculate e/m, in units of 10¹¹ C kg⁻¹, using e/m = 2V/(B²r²).', 2 * 250 / ((1.2e-3) ** 2 * 0.044 ** 2), '10¹¹ C kg⁻¹',
  f'e/m = 2V/(B²r²) = 2 × 250 ÷ ((1.2 × 10⁻³)² × 0.044²) = {sf(500 / (1.44e-6 * 0.044 ** 2))} C kg⁻¹', ['C1: formula', 'C1: substitution'], aq='e/m')
N('M', 3, 'Electrons accelerated through 200 V enter a field of 0.80 mT at right angles. Using e/m = 1.76 × 10¹¹ C kg⁻¹, calculate the radius of their path, in cm.', sqrt(2 * 200 / EM) / 0.80e-3 * 100, 'cm',
  f'r = √(2V/(e/m)) ÷ B = √(2 × 200 ÷ 1.76 × 10¹¹) ÷ 0.80 × 10⁻³ = {sf(sqrt(400 / EM) / 0.8e-3)} m. In cm: {sf(sqrt(400 / EM) / 0.8e-3, 4)} × 100 = {sf(sqrt(400 / EM) / 0.8e-3 * 100)}', ['M1: r = √(2mV/e)/B', 'C1: substitution'], aq='r')
M('M', 3, 'In a fine-beam tube at fixed B, why is the radius proportional to √V?', 'The speed is proportional to √V and the radius is proportional to the speed', ['The radius is proportional to V', 'The magnetic force is proportional to V', 'The charge increases with V'], '½mv² = eV gives v ∝ √V; r = mv/eB ∝ v.', ['C1: v ∝ √V', 'C1: r ∝ v'])
N('M', 4, 'Thomson\'s beam is undeflected with E = 2.0 × 10⁴ V m⁻¹ and B = 1.0 mT. With the electric field removed, the beam follows a circle of radius 11.4 cm. Calculate e/m, in units of 10¹¹ C kg⁻¹.', 2.0e4 / 1.0e-3 / (1.0e-3 * 0.114), '10¹¹ C kg⁻¹',
  f'v = E/B = 2.0 × 10⁴ ÷ 1.0 × 10⁻³ = 2.0 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹. e/m = v/(Br) = 2.0 × 10⁷ ÷ (1.0 × 10⁻³ × 0.114) = {sf(2e7 / (1e-3 * 0.114))} C kg⁻¹', ['C1: v = E/B', 'M1: e/m = v/(Br)', 'C1: value'], aq='e/m')
N('M', 3, 'Calculate the flux density needed to make electrons accelerated through 300 V follow a circle of radius 5.0 cm, in mT. Take e/m = 1.76 × 10¹¹ C kg⁻¹.', sqrt(2 * 300 / EM) / 0.050 * 1000, 'mT',
  f'B = √(2V/(e/m)) ÷ r = √(600 ÷ 1.76 × 10¹¹) ÷ 0.050 = {sf(sqrt(600 / EM) / 0.05)} T. In mT: {sf(sqrt(600 / EM) / 0.05, 4)} × 1000 = {sf(sqrt(600 / EM) / 0.05 * 1000)}', ['C1: rearranged', 'C1: conversion'], aq='B')
M('M', 3, 'In a fine-beam tube, the accelerating p.d. is increased while B stays the same. What happens to the circle?', 'Its radius increases', ['Its radius decreases', 'It stays the same', 'The beam goes straight'], 'Faster electrons need a larger circle: r ∝ √V.', ['C1: v increases', 'C1: r ∝ v'])
N('M', 3, 'An electron beam carries a current of 0.50 mA. Calculate the number of electrons passing a point each second.' + take('e'), 0.50e-3 / QE, 's⁻¹',
  f'N = I/e = 0.50 × 10⁻³ ÷ 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ = {sf(0.5e-3 / QE)} s⁻¹', ['C1: N = I/e', 'C1: substitution'], aq='N')
N('M', 3, 'Electrons pass undeflected through crossed fields at 3.0 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹. Calculate their kinetic energy, in keV.' + take('me', 'e'), 0.5 * ME * (3.0e7) ** 2 / QE / 1000, 'keV',
  f'E_k = 0.5 × 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ × (3.0 × 10⁷)² = {sf(0.5 * ME * 9e14)} J. In keV: {sf(0.5 * ME * 9e14, 4)} ÷ 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁶ = {sf(0.5 * ME * 9e14 / 1.6e-16)}', ['C1: ½mv²', 'C1: conversion to keV'], aq='E_k')
M('M', 4, 'Which observations convinced Thomson that cathode rays were streams of charged particles rather than waves?', 'They were deflected by electric and magnetic fields in the way expected for negative charges', ['They travelled in straight lines', 'They made the glass glow', 'They could pass through thin metal foil'], 'Deflection by both fields, consistent with F = eE and F = Bev, showed they were charged particles.', ['C1: electric deflection', 'C1: magnetic deflection', 'C1: negative charge'])
N('M', 3, 'Electrons moving at 2.0 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹ pass between plates 5.0 cm long where E = 1.0 × 10⁴ V m⁻¹. Using e/m = 1.76 × 10¹¹ C kg⁻¹, calculate their sideways deflection, in mm.', 0.5 * EM * 1.0e4 * (0.050 / 2.0e7) ** 2 * 1000, 'mm',
  f'a = (e/m)E = 1.76 × 10¹¹ × 1.0 × 10⁴ = 1.76 × 10¹⁵ m s⁻². t = 0.050 ÷ 2.0 × 10⁷ = 2.5 × 10⁻⁹ s. y = ½at² = 0.5 × 1.76 × 10¹⁵ × (2.5 × 10⁻⁹)² = {sf(0.5 * 1.76e15 * 6.25e-18)} m. In mm: {sf(0.5 * 1.76e15 * 6.25e-18, 4)} × 1000 = {sf(0.5 * 1.76e15 * 6.25e-18 * 1000)}', ['C1: a = (e/m)E', 'C1: y = ½at²'], aq='y')
N('M', 3, 'An oil drop carries a charge of 8.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ C. How many excess electrons does it carry?' + take('e'), 8.0e-19 / QE, None,
  'N = Q/e = 8.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ ÷ 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 5.0', ['C1: N = Q/e', 'C1: whole number'], sig=2, aq='N')
M('M', 3, 'How did combining Millikan\'s and Thomson\'s results give the mass of the electron?', 'Millikan found e and Thomson found e/m, so m = e ÷ (e/m)', ['Millikan found m directly', 'Thomson found m directly', 'Both found the speed of electrons'], 'Each experiment alone gives only e or e/m.', ['C1: e from Millikan', 'C1: e/m from Thomson'])
N('M', 4, 'Using classical mechanics, calculate the speed of electrons accelerated from rest through 5.0 kV, as a percentage of the speed of light. Take e/m = 1.76 × 10¹¹ C kg⁻¹.' + take('c'), sqrt(2 * EM * 5000) / 3.00e8 * 100, '%',
  f'v = √(2 × 1.76 × 10¹¹ × 5000) = {sf(sqrt(2 * EM * 5000))} m s⁻¹. Percentage = {sf(sqrt(2 * EM * 5000), 4)} ÷ 3.00 × 10⁸ × 100 = {sf(sqrt(2 * EM * 5000) / 3e8 * 100)} %', ['C1: v = √(2(e/m)V)', 'C1: ÷ c', 'C1: percentage'], aq='percentage')
N('M', 3, 'An electron gun accelerates a beam of 0.20 mA through 2.0 kV. Calculate the power delivered to the screen.', 0.20e-3 * 2.0e3, 'W',
  'P = IV = 0.20 × 10⁻³ × 2.0 × 10³ = 0.40 W', ['C1: each coulomb gains V joules', 'C1: P = IV'], sig=2, aq='P')
M('M', 3, 'In a fine-beam tube, the beam forms a complete circle only when the magnetic field is at right angles to the beam. What happens if the field is at an angle?', 'The beam follows a helix', ['The beam goes straight', 'The circle becomes larger', 'The beam stops'], 'The velocity component along the field is unaffected.', ['C1: parallel component unchanged', 'C1: helix'])
N('M', 3, 'Electrons accelerated through 250 V follow a circle of radius 6.0 cm in a field of 0.90 mT. Calculate e/m, in units of 10¹¹ C kg⁻¹.', 2 * 250 / ((0.90e-3) ** 2 * 0.060 ** 2), '10¹¹ C kg⁻¹',
  f'e/m = 2V/(B²r²) = 2 × 250 ÷ ((0.90 × 10⁻³)² × 0.060²) = {sf(500 / (8.1e-7 * 3.6e-3))} C kg⁻¹', ['C1: e/m = 2V/(B²r²)', 'C1: value'], aq='e/m')
M('M', 3, 'In a fine-beam tube, the direction of the magnetic field is reversed. What happens to the beam?', 'It curves the same amount but in the opposite direction', ['It goes straight', 'Its radius doubles', 'It disappears'], 'The force Bev reverses with B.', ['C1: force reverses', 'C1: same radius'])
N('M', 3, 'In Thomson\'s apparatus, plates 3.0 cm apart have a p.d. of 1500 V and the magnetic field is 1.5 mT. Calculate the speed of electrons that pass undeflected.', 1500 / 0.030 / 1.5e-3, 'm s⁻¹',
  f'E = 1500 ÷ 0.030 = 5.0 × 10⁴ V m⁻¹. v = E/B = 5.0 × 10⁴ ÷ 1.5 × 10⁻³ = {sf(5e4 / 1.5e-3)} m s⁻¹', ['C1: E = V/d', 'C1: v = E/B'], aq='v')
M('M', 3, 'Why must the cathode of an electron gun be heated?', 'To give electrons enough energy to escape from the metal surface', ['To make the anode positive', 'To create a magnetic field', 'To ionise the gas'], 'Electrons need energy at least equal to the work function to leave the metal.', ['C1: energy to escape', 'C1: thermionic emission'])
# hard
N('H', 6, 'In a fine-beam tube with a fixed field of 1.0 mT, the radius r is measured for different accelerating p.d.s V: V/V = 150, 200, 250, 300 and r/cm = 4.13, 4.77, 5.33, 5.84. (a) Show that r² = 2V/((e/m)B²). (b) Calculate the gradient of a graph of r² against V using the first and last points. (c) Calculate e/m, in units of 10¹¹ C kg⁻¹. Give only the answer to (c).', 2 / ((1.0e-3) ** 2 * ((0.0584 ** 2 - 0.0413 ** 2) / 150)), '10¹¹ C kg⁻¹',
  f'(a) eV = ½mv² and r = mv/(eB) give r² = 2mV/(eB²) = 2V/((e/m)B²). (b) Gradient = (0.0584² − 0.0413²) ÷ (300 − 150) = {sf((0.0584 ** 2 - 0.0413 ** 2) / 150)} m² V⁻¹. (c) e/m = 2 ÷ (B² × gradient) = 2 ÷ ((1.0 × 10⁻³)² × {sf((0.0584 ** 2 - 0.0413 ** 2) / 150, 4)}) = {sf(2 / (1e-6 * (0.0584 ** 2 - 0.0413 ** 2) / 150))} C kg⁻¹',
  ['B1: r² expression', 'C1: r in m', 'C1: gradient', 'M1: e/m = 2/(B² × gradient)', 'C1: value'], aq='e/m')
N('H', 6, 'In a Thomson-type experiment, plates 2.0 cm apart with a p.d. of 400 V and a field of 0.50 mT leave the beam undeflected. With the magnetic field switched off, the beam is deflected by 3.96 mm over the 6.0 cm length of the plates. (a) Calculate the speed of the electrons. (b) Show that e/m = 2yv²/(EL²). (c) Calculate e/m, in units of 10¹¹ C kg⁻¹. Give only the answer to (c).', 2 * 3.96e-3 * (400 / 0.020 / 0.50e-3) ** 2 / (400 / 0.020 * 0.060 ** 2), '10¹¹ C kg⁻¹',
  f'(a) E = 400 ÷ 0.020 = 2.0 × 10⁴ V m⁻¹; v = E/B = 2.0 × 10⁴ ÷ 0.50 × 10⁻³ = 4.0 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹. (b) y = ½(e/m)E(L/v)², so e/m = 2yv²/(EL²). (c) e/m = 2 × 3.96 × 10⁻³ × (4.0 × 10⁷)² ÷ (2.0 × 10⁴ × 0.060²) = {sf(2 * 3.96e-3 * 1.6e15 / (2e4 * 0.0036))} C kg⁻¹',
  ['C1: E = V/d', 'C1: v = E/B', 'M1: y = ½at² with a = (e/m)E', 'B1: rearranged', 'C1: value'], aq='e/m')
N('H', 5, 'Classical formulas for electron speed become inaccurate at high speeds. (a) Calculate the accelerating p.d. at which the classical speed of an electron would be 0.10c, in kV. Take e/m = 1.76 × 10¹¹ C kg⁻¹. (b) Suggest why a much higher p.d. is needed to reach 0.99c. Give only the answer to (a).' + take('c'), (0.10 * 3.00e8) ** 2 / (2 * EM) / 1000, 'kV',
  f'(a) v = 3.0 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹; V = v²/(2e/m) = (3.0 × 10⁷)² ÷ (2 × 1.76 × 10¹¹) = {sf(9e14 / (2 * EM))} V. In kV: {sf(9e14 / (2 * EM), 4)} ÷ 1000 = {sf(9e14 / (2 * EM) / 1000)}. (b) Near c the electron\'s effective mass grows and extra energy produces less and less increase in speed; it can never reach c.',
  ['C1: v = 0.10c', 'C1: V = v²/(2e/m)', 'C1: conversion', 'B1: relativistic effects near c'], aq='V')
N('H', 5, 'Thomson found that e/m for cathode-ray particles was 1.76 × 10¹¹ C kg⁻¹, while for hydrogen ions in electrolysis it was 9.58 × 10⁷ C kg⁻¹. (a) Calculate the ratio of the two values. (b) Assuming equal charges, state what this ratio means. (c) Use e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C to find the mass of the cathode-ray particle. Give only the answer to (a).', 1.76e11 / 9.58e7, None,
  f'(a) Ratio = 1.76 × 10¹¹ ÷ 9.58 × 10⁷ = {sf(1.76e11 / 9.58e7)}. (b) The particle has about 1/1840 of the mass of a hydrogen ion. (c) m = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ ÷ 1.76 × 10¹¹ = {sf(1.6e-19 / 1.76e11)} kg',
  ['C1: ratio', 'B1: mass ratio for equal charge', 'C1: m = e/(e/m)', 'C1: value'], aq='ratio')
N('H', 5, 'An oil drop of mass 9.6 × 10⁻¹⁵ kg is held stationary between plates 1.0 cm apart when the p.d. is 980 V. (a) Calculate the charge on the drop. (b) Calculate the number of excess electrons. (c) Explain how many such measurements showed that charge is quantised. Give only the answer to (b).' + take('g', 'e'), 9.6e-15 * g * 0.010 / 980 / QE, None,
  f'(a) QV/d = mg, so Q = 9.6 × 10⁻¹⁵ × 9.81 × 0.010 ÷ 980 = {sf(9.6e-15 * 9.81 * 0.01 / 980)} C. (b) N = {sf(9.6e-15 * 9.81 * 0.01 / 980, 4)} ÷ 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ = {sf(9.6e-15 * 9.81 * 0.01 / 980 / QE)}. (c) Every drop\'s charge was a whole-number multiple of the same value, 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.',
  ['M1: QV/d = mg', 'C1: Q', 'C1: N = Q/e', 'B1: all multiples of e'], aq='N')
N('H', 6, 'An electron gun has an accelerating p.d. of 4.0 kV and a beam current of 2.5 mA. (a) Calculate the number of electrons emitted per second. (b) Calculate the speed of the electrons, taking e/m = 1.76 × 10¹¹ C kg⁻¹. (c) Calculate the power delivered by the beam. (d) Suggest what happens to this energy at the screen. Give only the answer to (b).' + take('e'), sqrt(2 * EM * 4000), 'm s⁻¹',
  f'(a) N = 2.5 × 10⁻³ ÷ 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ = {sf(2.5e-3 / QE)} s⁻¹. (b) v = √(2 × 1.76 × 10¹¹ × 4000) = {sf(sqrt(2 * EM * 4000))} m s⁻¹. (c) P = IV = 2.5 × 10⁻³ × 4000 = 10 W. (d) Most becomes internal energy (heating) of the screen; a little becomes light.',
  ['C1: N = I/e', 'C1: v = √(2(e/m)V)', 'C1: P = IV', 'B1: heating and light', 'C1: value'], aq='v')
N('H', 5, 'In a fine-beam tube, electrons follow a circle of radius 6.0 cm. The accelerating p.d. and the magnetic field are then both doubled. (a) Show that r ∝ √V/B. (b) Calculate the new radius, in cm. Give only the answer to (b).', 6.0 * sqrt(2) / 2, 'cm',
  f'(a) r = √(2mV/e)/B, so r ∝ √V/B. (b) r = 6.0 × √2 ÷ 2 = {sf(6 * sqrt(2) / 2)} cm',
  ['M1: r = √(2mV/e)/B', 'B1: proportionality', 'C1: factor √2/2', 'C1: value'], aq='r')
N('H', 6, 'Electrons moving at 2.0 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹ cross a region 3.0 cm wide containing a field of 1.0 mT and are deflected through 15.3°. (a) Show that the radius of their path in the field is r = (width)/sin θ. (b) Calculate r. (c) Calculate e/m, in units of 10¹¹ C kg⁻¹. Give only the answer to (c).', 2.0e7 / (1.0e-3 * 0.030 / sin(radians(15.3))), '10¹¹ C kg⁻¹',
  f'(a) The electron follows an arc; the geometry of a circle crossing a strip of width d gives sin θ = d/r. (b) r = 0.030 ÷ sin 15.3° = {sf(0.03 / sin(radians(15.3)))} m. (c) e/m = v/(Br) = 2.0 × 10⁷ ÷ (1.0 × 10⁻³ × {sf(0.03 / sin(radians(15.3)), 4)}) = {sf(2e7 / (1e-3 * 0.03 / sin(radians(15.3))))} C kg⁻¹',
  ['B1: geometry', 'C1: r', 'M1: e/m = v/(Br)', 'C1: substitution', 'C1: value'], aq='e/m')
